 ## Overview
-The Dodecahedron Modulo method computes the signed distance to a regular dodecahedron by folding 3D space into a fundamental region using a sequence of reflections and rotations, then returning the distance to the boundary of that region [S1, unofficial]. It differs from analytical SDF approaches (which solve polynomial equations for the closest point on faces/edges) by relying on symmetry folding, which is often more compact to implement but requires careful handling of the folding sequence [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Basic Primitive Definitions](5346-basic-primitive-definitions.md).
+The Dodecahedron Modulo method computes the signed distance field (SDF) for a regular dodecahedron by folding 3D space into a single fundamental region using a sequence of reflections and rotations, then returning the distance to the boundary of that region [S1, unofficial]. It differs from analytical SDFs (which use algebraic minimization over faces/edges) by relying on symmetry folding, which is often more concise to implement but requires careful handling of the folding sequence [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Basic Primitive Definitions](5346-basic-primitive-definitions.md) [G].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** A 3D point $p = (x, y, z)$ in object space.
-**Outputs:** The signed distance $d$ from $p$ to the surface of a unit-radius dodecahedron centered at the origin.
+**Inputs and outputs:**
+- Input: `p` (vec3), the point in space to evaluate.
+- Output: `p` (vec3), the folded point. The SDF value is `length(p)` if the original point was outside, or `-length(p)` if inside, though the method primarily returns the folded coordinate for distance calculation [S1, unofficial].
 
-**Data Structures:**
-- `p`: A mutable 3D vector $(x, y, z)$.
-- Constants: $\phi = \frac{1+\sqrt{5}}{2}$ (Golden Ratio).
